===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|picalilli}}

===Etymology===
{{etydate|from 1845}}. {{unk|en}}. Presumably variant of {{m|en|pickle}},<ref name="mw">{{R:Merriam Webster Online}}</ref> which itself is from {{der|en|enm|pekille||spicy sauce served with meat or fish}} or {{m|enm|pikel}}, from {{der|en|dum|pekel||brine}}; perhaps influenced by [[dialectal]] variants.

Alternatively, may be related to {{m|en|paco lilla||ginger-spiced pickle}}, in 18th century British cookbook ''{{w|The Art of Cookery}}'' (1747–1843; e.g., 1805 edition) by {{w|Hannah Glasse}} (1708–1770), also referred to as “Indian pickles”,<ref>''{{w|The Art of Cookery}},'' {{w|Hannah Glasse}}, 1998 facsimile edition of 1805 edition, [http://books.google.com/books?id=BJY58UqSEMUC&pg=PA284&lpg=PA284&dq=%22paco+lilla%22 p. 284]</ref> which suggests an {{der|en|inc|-}} source; compare {{m|en|mulligatawny}}, of Tamil origin, which also features turmeric prominently, and {{m|en|curry}}, of Tamil or Middle English origin (or blend). No relation to {{m|en|Piccadilly}}.<ref name="mw" />

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|ɪli|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|British}} A [[yellow]] [[pickle]] [[relish]] made from [[cauliflower]], vegetable [[marrow]], and other vegetables, pickled with [[vinegar]], [[salt]], [[sugar]], and spiced with [[mustard]], [[turmeric]], and other spices.
#* {{quote-journal|en-GB|author=Sam Jones|title=Spanish writer spills beans on UK's saucy secrets|work=The Guardian|date=2020-04-09|url=https://www.theguardian.com/food/2020/apr/09/spanish-writer-spills-beans-on-uks-saucy-secrets|issn=0261-3077|passage=There are, however, exceptions. '''Piccalilli''' – aka “mustard with other stuff”, aka “that noble Anglo-Indian spiritual creation” – is great with ham, he conceeds{{sic}}.}}
# {{lb|en|US}} A [[pickle]], typically on a base of chopped green (unripe) tomatoes, but sometimes finely-chopped [[gherkin]]s, and possibly including other vegetables.

